1
00:00:12,829 --> 00:00:14,329
Thank you very much.

2
00:00:14,371 --> 00:00:18,371
Well, I would like to start with testicles.

3
00:00:18,663 --> 00:00:20,288
(Laughter)

4
00:00:21,204 --> 00:00:23,954
Men who sleep five hours a night

5
00:00:23,954 --> 00:00:29,204
have significantly smaller testicles than those who sleep seven hours or more.

6
00:00:29,246 --> 00:00:32,038
(Laughter)

7
00:00:32,079 --> 00:00:37,163
In addition, men who routinely sleep just four to five hours a night

8
00:00:37,163 --> 00:00:39,704
will have a level of testosterone

9
00:00:39,704 --> 00:00:43,038
which is that of someone 10 years their senior.

10
00:00:45,079 --> 00:00:48,829
So a lack of sleep will age a man by a decade

11
00:00:48,871 --> 00:00:51,662
in terms of that critical aspect of wellness.

12
00:00:53,954 --> 00:00:58,371
And we see equivalent impairments in female reproductive health

13
00:00:58,371 --> 00:00:59,787
caused by a lack of sleep.

14
00:01:03,079 --> 00:01:05,746
This is the best news that I have for you today.

15
00:01:05,746 --> 00:01:08,412
(Laughter)

16
00:01:08,454 --> 00:01:10,829
From this point, it may only get worse.

17
00:01:10,871 --> 00:01:13,621
Not only will I tell you about the wonderfully good things

18
00:01:13,662 --> 00:01:15,496
that happen when you get sleep,

19
00:01:15,537 --> 00:01:19,996
but the alarmingly bad things that happen when you don't get enough,

20
00:01:20,038 --> 00:01:22,538
both for your brain and for your body.

21
00:01:23,371 --> 00:01:25,788
Let me start with the brain

22
00:01:25,829 --> 00:01:28,829
and the functions of learning and memory,

23
00:01:28,871 --> 00:01:32,121
because what we've discovered over the past 10 or so years

24
00:01:32,163 --> 00:01:35,038
is that you need sleep after learning

25
00:01:35,079 --> 00:01:38,371
to essentially hit the save button on those new memories

26
00:01:38,371 --> 00:01:39,663
so that you don't forget.

27
00:01:40,954 --> 00:01:46,454
But recently, we discovered that you also need sleep before learning

28
00:01:46,496 --> 00:01:49,538
to actually prepare your brain,

29
00:01:49,579 --> 00:01:51,621
almost like a dry sponge

30
00:01:51,663 --> 00:01:54,121
ready to initially soak up new information.

31
00:01:55,163 --> 00:01:58,288
And without sleep, the memory circuits of the brain

32
00:01:58,288 --> 00:02:01,079
essentially become waterlogged, as it were,

33
00:02:01,079 --> 00:02:03,246
and you can't absorb new memories.

34
00:02:04,163 --> 00:02:06,079
So let me show you the data.

35
00:02:06,079 --> 00:02:10,329
Here in this study, we decided to test the hypothesis

36
00:02:10,371 --> 00:02:12,913
that pulling the all-nighter was a good idea.

37
00:02:14,246 --> 00:02:16,579
So we took a group of individuals

38
00:02:16,621 --> 00:02:20,163
and we assigned them to one of two experimental groups:

39
00:02:20,204 --> 00:02:23,663
a sleep group and a sleep deprivation group.

40
00:02:23,663 --> 00:02:28,288
Now the sleep group, they're going to get a full eight hours of slumber,

41
00:02:28,329 --> 00:02:30,996
but the deprivation group, we're going to keep them awake

42
00:02:30,996 --> 00:02:33,163
in the laboratory, under full supervision.

43
00:02:33,663 --> 00:02:38,038
There's no naps or caffeine, by the way, so it's miserable for everyone involved.

44
00:02:38,579 --> 00:02:40,121
And then the next day,

45
00:02:40,121 --> 00:02:44,454
we're going to place those participants inside an MRI scanner

46
00:02:44,454 --> 00:02:48,246
and we're going to have them try and learn a whole list of new facts

47
00:02:48,288 --> 00:02:51,579
as we're taking snapshots of brain activity.

48
00:02:51,579 --> 00:02:53,579
And then we're going to test them

49
00:02:53,579 --> 00:02:56,079
to see how effective that learning has been.

50
00:02:56,954 --> 00:02:59,871
And that's what you're looking at here on the vertical axis.

51
00:03:00,496 --> 00:03:03,204
And when you put those two groups head to head,

52
00:03:03,204 --> 00:03:08,246
what you find is a quite significant, 40-percent deficit

53
00:03:08,288 --> 00:03:11,746
in the ability of the brain to make new memories without sleep.

54
00:03:13,038 --> 00:03:14,871
I think this should be concerning,

55
00:03:14,913 --> 00:03:17,121
considering what we know is happening to sleep

56
00:03:17,163 --> 00:03:19,579
in our education populations right now.

57
00:03:20,538 --> 00:03:22,371
In fact, to put that in context,

58
00:03:22,371 --> 00:03:25,871
it would be the difference in a child acing an exam

59
00:03:25,913 --> 00:03:28,788
versus failing it miserably -- 40 percent.

60
00:03:30,288 --> 00:03:34,496
And we've gone on to discover what goes wrong within your brain

61
00:03:34,496 --> 00:03:37,621
to produce these types of learning disabilities.

62
00:03:38,746 --> 00:03:40,329
And there's a structure that sits

63
00:03:40,371 --> 00:03:44,996
on the left and the right side of your brain, called the hippocampus.

64
00:03:44,996 --> 00:03:46,788
And you can think of the hippocampus

65
00:03:46,829 --> 00:03:50,038
almost like the informational inbox of your brain.

66
00:03:50,621 --> 00:03:53,829
It's very good at receiving new memory files

67
00:03:53,829 --> 00:03:55,329
and then holding on to them.

68
00:03:56,913 --> 00:03:58,621
And when you look at this structure

69
00:03:58,663 --> 00:04:01,704
in those people who'd had a full night of sleep,

70
00:04:01,746 --> 00:04:05,288
we saw lots of healthy learning-related activity.

71
00:04:06,621 --> 00:04:09,579
Yet in those people who were sleep-deprived,

72
00:04:09,621 --> 00:04:13,246
we actually couldn't find any significant signal whatsoever.

73
00:04:14,746 --> 00:04:19,704
So it's almost as though sleep deprivation had shut down your memory inbox,

74
00:04:19,746 --> 00:04:22,704
and any new incoming files -- they were just being bounced.

75
00:04:23,913 --> 00:04:27,496
You couldn't effectively commit new experiences to memory.

76
00:04:30,038 --> 00:04:34,538
So that's the bad that can happen if I were to take sleep away from you,

77
00:04:34,579 --> 00:04:37,663
but let me just come back to that control group for a second.

78
00:04:38,454 --> 00:04:41,788
Do you remember those folks that got a full eight hours of sleep?

79
00:04:42,413 --> 00:04:44,871
Well, we can ask a very different question:

80
00:04:44,913 --> 00:04:48,538
What is it about the physiological quality of your sleep

81
00:04:48,538 --> 00:04:50,079
when you do get it

82
00:04:50,121 --> 00:04:53,913
that restores and enhances your memory and learning ability

83
00:04:53,954 --> 00:04:55,079
each and every day?

84
00:04:55,996 --> 00:04:58,996
And by placing electrodes all over the head,

85
00:04:58,996 --> 00:05:03,038
what we've discovered is that there are big, powerful brainwaves

86
00:05:03,079 --> 00:05:06,704
that happen during the very deepest stages of sleep

87
00:05:06,704 --> 00:05:08,996
that have riding on top of them

88
00:05:09,038 --> 00:05:12,288
these spectacular bursts of electrical activity

89
00:05:12,329 --> 00:05:14,079
that we call sleep spindles.

90
00:05:15,079 --> 00:05:19,496
And it's the combined quality of these deep-sleep brainwaves

91
00:05:19,496 --> 00:05:23,954
that acts like a file-transfer mechanism at night,

92
00:05:23,996 --> 00:05:28,079
shifting memories from a short-term vulnerable reservoir

93
00:05:28,079 --> 00:05:32,413
to a more permanent long-term storage site within the brain,

94
00:05:32,454 --> 00:05:35,829
and therefore protecting them, making them safe.

95
00:05:37,454 --> 00:05:39,496
And it is important that we understand

96
00:05:39,538 --> 00:05:44,579
what during sleep actually transacts these memory benefits,

97
00:05:44,621 --> 00:05:48,746
because there are real medical and societal implications.

98
00:05:48,788 --> 00:05:50,788
And let me just tell you about one area

99
00:05:50,788 --> 00:05:54,246
that we've moved this work out into, clinically,

100
00:05:54,288 --> 00:05:57,496
which is the context of aging and dementia.

101
00:05:58,871 --> 00:06:02,288
Because it's of course no secret that, as we get older,

102
00:06:02,329 --> 00:06:05,579
our learning and memory abilities begin to fade and decline.

103
00:06:07,038 --> 00:06:08,663
But what we've also discovered

104
00:06:08,704 --> 00:06:15,454
is that a physiological signature of aging is that your sleep gets worse,

105
00:06:15,496 --> 00:06:19,288
especially that deep quality of sleep that I was just discussing.

106
00:06:20,621 --> 00:06:23,746
And only last year, we finally published evidence

107
00:06:23,746 --> 00:06:26,704
that these two things, they're not simply co-occurring,

108
00:06:26,746 --> 00:06:29,538
they are significantly interrelated.

109
00:06:30,829 --> 00:06:34,121
And it suggests that the disruption of deep sleep

110
00:06:34,163 --> 00:06:36,454
is an underappreciated factor

111
00:06:36,454 --> 00:06:40,371
that is contributing to cognitive decline or memory decline

112
00:06:40,413 --> 00:06:43,996
in aging, and most recently we've discovered,

113
00:06:43,996 --> 00:06:46,204
in Alzheimer's disease as well.

114
00:06:48,329 --> 00:06:52,079
Now, I know this is remarkably depressing news.

115
00:06:52,079 --> 00:06:54,371
It's in the mail. It's coming at you.

116
00:06:54,413 --> 00:06:56,704
But there's a potential silver lining here.

117
00:06:57,746 --> 00:07:02,621
Unlike many of the other factors that we know are associated with aging,

118
00:07:02,621 --> 00:07:06,288
for example changes in the physical structure of the brain,

119
00:07:06,329 --> 00:07:09,371
that's fiendishly difficult to treat.

120
00:07:09,413 --> 00:07:13,954
But that sleep is a missing piece in the explanatory puzzle

121
00:07:13,996 --> 00:07:16,579
of aging and Alzheimer's is exciting

122
00:07:16,621 --> 00:07:19,329
because we may be able to do something about it.

123
00:07:20,538 --> 00:07:24,454
And one way that we are approaching this at my sleep center

124
00:07:24,496 --> 00:07:27,496
is not by using sleeping pills, by the way.

125
00:07:27,496 --> 00:07:32,454
Unfortunately, they are blunt instruments that do not produce naturalistic sleep.

126
00:07:33,538 --> 00:07:36,788
Instead, we're actually developing a method based on this.

127
00:07:36,788 --> 00:07:39,996
It's called direct current brain stimulation.

128
00:07:40,038 --> 00:07:43,329
You insert a small amount of voltage into the brain,

129
00:07:43,329 --> 00:07:45,579
so small you typically don't feel it,

130
00:07:45,621 --> 00:07:47,496
but it has a measurable impact.

131
00:07:48,829 --> 00:07:55,163
Now if you apply this stimulation during sleep in young, healthy adults,

132
00:07:55,204 --> 00:07:59,913
as if you're sort of singing in time with those deep-sleep brainwaves,

133
00:07:59,913 --> 00:08:04,454
not only can you amplify the size of those deep-sleep brainwaves,

134
00:08:04,496 --> 00:08:09,038
but in doing so, we can almost double the amount of memory benefit

135
00:08:09,079 --> 00:08:11,079
that you get from sleep.

136
00:08:11,121 --> 00:08:13,913
The question now is whether we can translate

137
00:08:13,954 --> 00:08:18,246
this same affordable, potentially portable piece of technology

138
00:08:18,288 --> 00:08:21,163
into older adults and those with dementia.

139
00:08:22,704 --> 00:08:27,288
Can we restore back some healthy quality of deep sleep,

140
00:08:27,329 --> 00:08:31,954
and in doing so, can we salvage aspects of their learning

141
00:08:31,954 --> 00:08:33,454
and memory function?

142
00:08:33,496 --> 00:08:35,663
That is my real hope now.

143
00:08:36,371 --> 00:08:39,371
That's one of our moon-shot goals, as it were.

144
00:08:41,329 --> 00:08:44,704
So that's an example of sleep for your brain,

145
00:08:44,704 --> 00:08:47,912
but sleep is just as essential for your body.

146
00:08:49,287 --> 00:08:52,954
We've already spoken about sleep loss and your reproductive system.

147
00:08:53,871 --> 00:08:58,704
Or I could tell you about sleep loss and your cardiovascular system,

148
00:08:58,746 --> 00:09:01,912
and that all it takes is one hour.

149
00:09:01,954 --> 00:09:07,454
Because there is a global experiment performed on 1.6 billion people

150
00:09:07,454 --> 00:09:11,746
across 70 countries twice a year,

151
00:09:11,787 --> 00:09:13,662
and it's called daylight saving time.

152
00:09:14,912 --> 00:09:18,371
Now, in the spring, when we lose one hour of sleep,

153
00:09:18,371 --> 00:09:23,996
we see a subsequent 24-percent increase in heart attacks that following day.

154
00:09:26,954 --> 00:09:30,121
In the autumn, when we gain an hour of sleep,

155
00:09:30,162 --> 00:09:33,996
we see a 21-percent reduction in heart attacks.

156
00:09:35,746 --> 00:09:37,412
Isn't that incredible?

157
00:09:38,912 --> 00:09:44,621
And you see exactly the same profile for car crashes, road traffic accidents,

158
00:09:44,621 --> 00:09:46,537
even suicide rates.

159
00:09:48,912 --> 00:09:51,829
But as a deeper dive, I want to focus on this:

160
00:09:51,871 --> 00:09:54,329
sleep loss and your immune system.

161
00:09:55,162 --> 00:09:59,996
And here, I'll introduce these delightful blue elements in the image.

162
00:09:59,996 --> 00:10:03,204
They are called natural killer cells,

163
00:10:03,204 --> 00:10:08,079
and you can think of natural killer cells almost like the secret service agents

164
00:10:08,079 --> 00:10:09,704
of your immune system.

165
00:10:09,704 --> 00:10:14,704
They are very good at identifying dangerous, unwanted elements

166
00:10:14,746 --> 00:10:16,121
and eliminating them.

167
00:10:17,246 --> 00:10:21,204
In fact, what they're doing here is destroying a cancerous tumor mass.

168
00:10:22,579 --> 00:10:28,537
So what you wish for is a virile set of these immune assassins

169
00:10:28,537 --> 00:10:30,579
at all times,

170
00:10:30,621 --> 00:10:34,412
and tragically, that's what you don't have if you're not sleeping enough.

171
00:10:35,829 --> 00:10:37,454
So here in this experiment,

172
00:10:37,496 --> 00:10:41,329
you're not going to have your sleep deprived for an entire night,

173
00:10:41,329 --> 00:10:44,871
you're simply going to have your sleep restricted to four hours

174
00:10:44,871 --> 00:10:46,662
for one single night,

175
00:10:46,662 --> 00:10:49,662
and then we're going to look to see what's the percent reduction

176
00:10:49,704 --> 00:10:51,704
in immune cell activity that you suffer.

177
00:10:52,787 --> 00:10:55,579
And it's not small -- it's not 10 percent,

178
00:10:55,621 --> 00:10:57,621
it's not 20 percent.

179
00:10:57,662 --> 00:11:02,204
There was a 70-percent drop in natural killer cell activity.

180
00:11:03,787 --> 00:11:07,829
That's a concerning state of immune deficiency,

181
00:11:09,371 --> 00:11:12,162
and you can perhaps understand why we're now finding

182
00:11:12,204 --> 00:11:16,037
significant links between short sleep duration

183
00:11:16,079 --> 00:11:19,996
and your risk for the development of numerous forms of cancer.

184
00:11:21,871 --> 00:11:24,662
Currently, that list includes cancer of the bowel,

185
00:11:24,704 --> 00:11:27,954
cancer of the prostate and cancer of the breast.

186
00:11:29,829 --> 00:11:35,204
In fact, the link between a lack of sleep and cancer is now so strong

187
00:11:35,246 --> 00:11:37,621
that the World Health Organization

188
00:11:37,621 --> 00:11:41,829
has classified any form of nighttime shift work

189
00:11:41,829 --> 00:11:45,037
as a probable carcinogen,

190
00:11:45,037 --> 00:11:47,704
because of a disruption of your sleep-wake rhythms.

191
00:11:49,829 --> 00:11:52,537
So you may have heard of that old maxim

192
00:11:52,579 --> 00:11:54,704
that you can sleep when you're dead.

193
00:11:54,704 --> 00:11:56,787
Well, I'm being quite serious now --

194
00:11:56,829 --> 00:11:59,412
it is mortally unwise advice.

195
00:12:00,371 --> 00:12:05,412
We know this from epidemiological studies across millions of individuals.

196
00:12:05,412 --> 00:12:07,412
There's a simple truth:

197
00:12:07,454 --> 00:12:10,829
the shorter your sleep, the shorter your life.

198
00:12:10,871 --> 00:12:14,412
Short sleep predicts all-cause mortality.

199
00:12:16,954 --> 00:12:21,121
And if increasing your risk for the development of cancer

200
00:12:21,121 --> 00:12:23,371
or even Alzheimer's disease

201
00:12:23,412 --> 00:12:27,579
were not sufficiently disquieting,

202
00:12:27,579 --> 00:12:31,287
we have since discovered that a lack of sleep will even erode

203
00:12:31,329 --> 00:12:35,412
the very fabric of biological life itself,

204
00:12:36,662 --> 00:12:38,662
your DNA genetic code.

205
00:12:40,037 --> 00:12:43,954
So here in this study, they took a group of healthy adults

206
00:12:43,996 --> 00:12:47,746
and they limited them to six hours of sleep a night

207
00:12:47,746 --> 00:12:49,412
for one week,

208
00:12:49,412 --> 00:12:53,246
and then they measured the change in their gene activity profile

209
00:12:53,246 --> 00:12:55,704
relative to when those same individuals

210
00:12:55,704 --> 00:12:58,287
were getting a full eight hours of sleep a night.

211
00:12:59,454 --> 00:13:01,246
And there were two critical findings.

212
00:13:02,204 --> 00:13:07,287
First, a sizable and significant 711 genes

213
00:13:07,287 --> 00:13:09,621
were distorted in their activity,

214
00:13:09,662 --> 00:13:11,079
caused by a lack of sleep.

215
00:13:11,912 --> 00:13:15,037
The second result was that about half of those genes

216
00:13:15,079 --> 00:13:17,496
were actually increased in their activity.

217
00:13:17,829 --> 00:13:19,787
The other half were decreased.

218
00:13:20,454 --> 00:13:23,704
Now those genes that were switched off by a lack of sleep

219
00:13:23,746 --> 00:13:27,246
were genes associated with your immune system,

220
00:13:27,246 --> 00:13:30,121
so once again, you can see that immune deficiency.

221
00:13:31,329 --> 00:13:34,412
In contrast, those genes that were actually upregulated

222
00:13:34,454 --> 00:13:36,829
or increased by way of a lack of sleep,

223
00:13:36,829 --> 00:13:40,746
were genes associated with the promotion of tumors,

224
00:13:40,746 --> 00:13:45,912
genes associated with long-term chronic inflammation within the body,

225
00:13:45,954 --> 00:13:48,746
and genes associated with stress,

226
00:13:48,746 --> 00:13:51,787
and, as a consequence, cardiovascular disease.

227
00:13:53,162 --> 00:13:56,829
There is simply no aspect of your wellness

228
00:13:56,871 --> 00:14:00,121
that can retreat at the sign of sleep deprivation

229
00:14:00,162 --> 00:14:01,954
and get away unscathed.

230
00:14:02,912 --> 00:14:05,912
It's rather like a broken water pipe in your home.

231
00:14:05,954 --> 00:14:09,579
Sleep loss will leak down into every nook and cranny

232
00:14:09,621 --> 00:14:11,996
of your physiology,

233
00:14:12,037 --> 00:14:15,912
even tampering with the very DNA nucleic alphabet

234
00:14:15,954 --> 00:14:18,787
that spells out your daily health narrative.

235
00:14:21,871 --> 00:14:24,537
And at this point, you may be thinking,

236
00:14:24,579 --> 00:14:27,121
"Oh my goodness, how do I start to get better sleep?

237
00:14:27,121 --> 00:14:29,371
What are you tips for good sleep?"

238
00:14:30,079 --> 00:14:34,537
Well, beyond avoiding the damaging and harmful impact

239
00:14:34,579 --> 00:14:37,496
of alcohol and caffeine on sleep,

240
00:14:37,537 --> 00:14:39,954
and if you're struggling with sleep at night,

241
00:14:39,996 --> 00:14:42,162
avoiding naps during the day,

242
00:14:42,204 --> 00:14:44,329
I have two pieces of advice for you.

243
00:14:45,079 --> 00:14:47,912
The first is regularity.

244
00:14:47,954 --> 00:14:51,329
Go to bed at the same time, wake up at the same time,

245
00:14:51,371 --> 00:14:54,412
no matter whether it's the weekday or the weekend.

246
00:14:54,454 --> 00:14:57,204
Regularity is king,

247
00:14:57,204 --> 00:14:59,162
and it will anchor your sleep

248
00:14:59,204 --> 00:15:02,496
and improve the quantity and the quality of that sleep.

249
00:15:04,371 --> 00:15:07,037
The second is keep it cool.

250
00:15:08,079 --> 00:15:10,496
Your body needs to drop its core temperature

251
00:15:10,537 --> 00:15:14,746
by about two to three degrees Fahrenheit to initiate sleep

252
00:15:14,787 --> 00:15:16,871
and then to stay asleep,

253
00:15:16,871 --> 00:15:19,371
and it's the reason you will always find it easier

254
00:15:19,412 --> 00:15:21,787
to fall asleep in a room that's too cold

255
00:15:21,787 --> 00:15:23,037
than too hot.

256
00:15:23,996 --> 00:15:27,537
So aim for a bedroom temperature of around 65 degrees,

257
00:15:27,537 --> 00:15:29,496
or about 18 degrees Celsius.

258
00:15:29,537 --> 00:15:32,287
That's going to be optimal for the sleep of most people.

259
00:15:34,454 --> 00:15:37,954
And then finally, in taking a step back, then,

260
00:15:37,954 --> 00:15:41,162
what is the mission-critical statement here?

261
00:15:42,787 --> 00:15:44,996
Well, I think it may be this:

262
00:15:44,996 --> 00:15:50,287
sleep, unfortunately, is not an optional lifestyle luxury.

263
00:15:51,121 --> 00:15:55,662
Sleep is a nonnegotiable biological necessity.

264
00:15:56,871 --> 00:16:00,496
It is your life-support system,

265
00:16:00,496 --> 00:16:05,121
and it is Mother Nature's best effort yet at immortality.

266
00:16:06,704 --> 00:16:11,204
And the decimation of sleep throughout industrialized nations

267
00:16:11,246 --> 00:16:16,454
is having a catastrophic impact on our health, our wellness,

268
00:16:16,496 --> 00:16:19,162
even the safety and the education of our children.

269
00:16:20,329 --> 00:16:23,329
It's a silent sleep loss epidemic,

270
00:16:23,371 --> 00:16:26,704
and it's fast becoming one of the greatest public health challenges

271
00:16:26,746 --> 00:16:29,454
that we face in the 21st century.

272
00:16:32,287 --> 00:16:37,371
I believe it is now time for us to reclaim our right

273
00:16:37,412 --> 00:16:38,871
to a full night of sleep,

274
00:16:39,996 --> 00:16:42,162
and without embarrassment

275
00:16:42,204 --> 00:16:45,579
or that unfortunate stigma of laziness.

276
00:16:46,621 --> 00:16:52,621
And in doing so, we can be reunited with the most powerful elixir of life,

277
00:16:54,037 --> 00:16:56,746
the Swiss Army knife of health, as it were.

278
00:16:58,662 --> 00:17:01,287
And with that soapbox rant over,

279
00:17:01,329 --> 00:17:03,412
I will simply say, good night, good luck,

280
00:17:03,454 --> 00:17:04,579
and above all ...

281
00:17:06,871 --> 00:17:08,662
I do hope you sleep well.

282
00:17:08,662 --> 00:17:10,037
Thank you very much indeed.

283
00:17:10,037 --> 00:17:14,579
(Applause)

284
00:17:14,621 --> 00:17:15,746
Thank you.

285
00:17:15,787 --> 00:17:18,621
(Applause)

286
00:17:18,663 --> 00:17:20,204
Thank you so much.

287
00:17:20,204 --> 00:17:22,954
David Biello: No, no, no. Stay there for a second.

288
00:17:22,996 --> 00:17:25,538
Good job not running away, though. I appreciate that.

289
00:17:25,579 --> 00:17:26,829
So that was terrifying.

290
00:17:26,829 --> 00:17:29,913
Matt Walker: You're welcome. DB: Yes, thank you, thank you.

291
00:17:30,329 --> 00:17:35,496
Since we can't catch up on sleep, what are we supposed to do?

292
00:17:35,496 --> 00:17:39,038
What do we do when we're, like, tossing and turning in bed late at night

293
00:17:39,079 --> 00:17:42,121
or doing shift work or whatever else?

294
00:17:42,163 --> 00:17:44,496
MW: So you're right, we can't catch up on sleep.

295
00:17:44,538 --> 00:17:45,829
Sleep is not like the bank.

296
00:17:45,871 --> 00:17:47,913
You can't accumulate a debt

297
00:17:47,913 --> 00:17:50,621
and then hope to pay it off at a later point in time.

298
00:17:50,663 --> 00:17:53,788
I should also note the reason that it's so catastrophic

299
00:17:53,829 --> 00:17:56,871
and that our health deteriorates so quickly,

300
00:17:56,913 --> 00:17:59,621
first, it's because human beings are the only species

301
00:17:59,663 --> 00:18:02,121
that deliberately deprive themselves of sleep

302
00:18:02,163 --> 00:18:04,538
for no apparent reason.

303
00:18:04,579 --> 00:18:05,704
DB: Because we're smart.

304
00:18:05,746 --> 00:18:10,579
MW: And I make that point because it means that Mother Nature,

305
00:18:10,579 --> 00:18:12,288
throughout the course of evolution,

306
00:18:12,288 --> 00:18:16,621
has never had to face the challenge of this thing called sleep deprivation.

307
00:18:17,288 --> 00:18:20,704
So she's never developed a safety net,

308
00:18:20,746 --> 00:18:22,871
and that's why when you undersleep,

309
00:18:22,871 --> 00:18:27,204
things just sort of implode so quickly, both within the brain and the body.

310
00:18:27,246 --> 00:18:29,996
So you just have to prioritize.

311
00:18:30,038 --> 00:18:33,329
DB: OK, but tossing and turning in bed,

312
00:18:33,371 --> 00:18:34,788
what do I do?

313
00:18:34,788 --> 00:18:39,413
MW: So if you are staying in bed awake for too long,

314
00:18:39,454 --> 00:18:42,704
you should get out of bed and go to a different room

315
00:18:42,704 --> 00:18:44,038
and do something different.

316
00:18:44,038 --> 00:18:48,371
The reason is because your brain will very quickly associate your bedroom

317
00:18:48,413 --> 00:18:50,704
with the place of wakefulness,

318
00:18:50,746 --> 00:18:53,413
and you need to break that association.

319
00:18:53,454 --> 00:18:56,329
So only return to bed when you are sleepy,

320
00:18:56,371 --> 00:18:59,913
and that way you will relearn the association that you once had,

321
00:18:59,954 --> 00:19:02,454
which is your bed is the place of sleep.

322
00:19:02,454 --> 00:19:04,121
So the analogy would be,

323
00:19:04,163 --> 00:19:07,746
you'd never sit at the dinner table, waiting to get hungry,

324
00:19:07,746 --> 00:19:10,704
so why would you lie in bed, waiting to get sleepy?

325
00:19:11,538 --> 00:19:13,538
DB: Well, thank you for that wake-up call.

326
00:19:13,579 --> 00:19:14,746
Great job, Matt.

327
00:19:14,746 --> 00:19:16,954
MW: You're very welcome. Thank you very much.
